Q: Minor difficulties with Ajax and PHP interaction A: <p>Hi the skinny is you need 3 parts to make ajax work,</p>

<ul>
<li>serverside code to generate the page</li>
<li>ajax ( clientside ) to make the call and respond</li>
<li>seperate serverside to receive it.</li>
</ul>

<p>Also it will be easier to replace the table completely then to pick out elements.  But that is up to you.</p>

<p>So say we have the page with our ajax call</p>

<pre><code> &lt;script type="text/javascript" &gt;
 $(document).ready(function() {
    $(".order-by-select").click(function() {var orderValue = this.value;
    $.post("&lt;?php echo base_url() ?&gt;welcome/index", {val: orderValue}, function(data) {
        alert(data);
    });
});
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Now you seem to have some json response I'll assume you get this from the alert above;</p>

<pre><code> [{"id":"1","name":"Nick","age":"18"},{"id":"2","name":"John","age":"23"}]
</code></pre>

<p>I'll also assume that this comes from something like </p>

<pre><code>echo json_encode( array( array('id'=&gt;1, ...), array('id'=&gt;2 ...) .. ); 
</code></pre>

<p>It's important before doing the echo that you tell the server that this is json, you do this using a header, but you cannot output anything before the header, and after the json header all output must be in the json format or it wont work, it's like telling the browser that this is html, or an image etc. what the content is.</p>

<pre><code>  Header("Content-Type: application/json");
  echo json_encode( ....
</code></pre>

<p>You can get away without doing this sometimes, but often you'll need to use eval or something, by telling the browser its json you don't need that.  Now doing an alert is great and all but if you see the string data <code>[{"id": ..</code> your header is wrong, you should get something like <code>[object]</code> when you do the alert.</p>

<p>No once we have a factual Json object we can make use of all that wonderful data</p>

<pre><code>&lt;script type="text/javascript" &gt;
 $(document).ready(function() {
    $(".order-by-select").click(function() {var orderValue = this.value;
    $.post("&lt;?php echo base_url() ?&gt;welcome/index", {val: orderValue}, function(data) {
        $.each(data, function(i,v){
            alert(v.id);
            alert(v.name);
        });
    });
});
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>This should loop through all the data and do 2 alerts, first the id then the name, right.  Next it's a simple matter of replacing the content using <code>.text()</code> or <code>.append()</code></p>

<pre><code>  &lt;script type="text/javascript" &gt;
 $(document).ready(function() {
    $(".order-by-select").click(function() {var orderValue = this.value;
    $.post("&lt;?php echo base_url() ?&gt;welcome/index", {val: orderValue}, function(data) {
        $.each(data, function(i,v){
           $('#test').append('&lt;p&gt;'+v.id+'&lt;/p&gt;');
        });
    });
});
&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;p id="test" &gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How can I return an array of strings that matches the web data it&#39;s scraping off of? A: <p>You are very close. The issue is probably not with <code>textContent</code>; it is more likely that the page has not finished expanding the last review before you extract the text.</p>
<p>This loop is a little fragile:</p>
<pre><code>let revealsRemaining = await firstPage.locator('a.reveal').count();

while (revealsRemaining &gt; 0) {
  let revealLink = firstPage.locator('a.reveal').first();
  await revealLink.click();
  revealsRemaining--;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The reason is that you count the links once, then keep clicking the first <code>a.reveal</code>. But after each click, Letterboxd changes the DOM. The clicked link may be removed, hidden, or replaced, and the review text may update asynchronously.</p>
<p>Playwright waits until the click can happen, but it does not automatically know that the review has finished expanding. You need to wait for the result of the click before moving on.</p>
<p>Replace your current reveal-clicking loop with this, then run the text extraction after it finishes:</p>
<pre><code>const reveals = firstPage.locator('a.reveal');

while (await reveals.count() &gt; 0) {
  const reveal = reveals.first();

  await reveal.scrollIntoViewIfNeeded();
  await reveal.click();

  await reveal.waitFor({ state: 'detached' }).catch(() =&gt;
    reveal.waitFor({ state: 'hidden' })
  );
}

const pageOneReviews = await firstPage
  .locator('.js-listitem .js-review .body-text')
  .evaluateAll((els) =&gt;
    els.map((el) =&gt;
      Array.from(el.querySelectorAll('p'))
        .map((p) =&gt; p.textContent.trim())
        .filter(Boolean)
        .join('\n\n')
    )
  );
</code></pre>
<p>If Letterboxd does not remove or hide the reveal link and only updates the review body, wait for the number of reveal links to decrease instead:</p>
<pre><code>const reveals = firstPage.locator('a.reveal');

while (true) {
  const before = await reveals.count();

  if (before === 0) break;

  await reveals.first().scrollIntoViewIfNeeded();
  await reveals.first().click();

  await firstPage.waitForFunction((count) =&gt; {
    return document.querySelectorAll('a.reveal').length &lt; count;
  }, before);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The main idea is:</p>
<pre><code>click &quot;...more&quot;
wait until that review is actually expanded
repeat until no reveal links remain
then extract the text
</code></pre>
<p>So the last review is probably being clicked, but your extraction is happening before Letterboxd has finished updating the DOM. Waiting for the page-specific result of the click should make the scraper much more reliable.</p>

Q: How to clear input after onClick with ReactJS A: <p>If you're just trying to clear some form fields, you could set the state for each field after submission to <code>''</code>.</p>

<p>For example: </p>

<pre><code>this.setState({
  amount: '', 
  exList: ''
});
</code></pre>

<p>You would add this after all of your data has been saved and processed, so at the end of your function would be ok. Or, you could create another function to handle clearing each form field.</p>
